--TEST--
`Core\Fatal::onLimit` registered a thousand times holds one handler, and the thousand it replaced are released — `rule:errors/on-limit`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// § 1 gives the tier *one* handler, and the consequence that is measurable
// before anything fires is a memory one: each registration replaces the last,
// so the reference the context dropped has to go with it. A registration that
// only ever retained — the easy half of `Ctx::set_limit_handler` to write —
// leaks one callable per call and passes every case that counts what ran.
//
// Asserted by counting rather than read off a line: the loop registers a fresh
// capturing anonymous function each turn under a ceiling far below what a thousand leaked
// ones would hold, so a tree that leaks them does not print a wrong number, it
// dies at `rule:errors/on-limit`'s `FATAL` partway through.
int $registered = 0;
while ($registered < 1000) {
    var $slab = Core\Str::repeat("x", 16384);
    Core\Fatal::onLimit(fn (): void => { echo Core\Str::length($slab), "\n"; });
    $registered++;
}
echo "registered=", $registered, "\n";

// Still exactly one handler in force, and still not run: the thousand callables
// above captured a 16 KiB string each and none of them printed it.
Core\Fatal::onLimit(fn (): void => { echo "the last one\n"; });
echo "done\n";
--FILE nvs.toml--
# Comfortably above what one request needs and far below the ~16 MiB a thousand
# leaked captures would hold. `rule:config/ini-set-is-core-config-set` is the unit.
[limits]
memory = "8M"
--EXPECT--
registered=1000
done
